<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Twelver Shi'ism</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Twelver Shiʿism</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Twelver <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiism">Shi'ism</a> is the largest branch of Shia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, recognising a line of twelve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imams</a>, the last of whom is believed to be in occultation. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> profile notes that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhan">Ilkhan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oljeitu">Öljeitü</a>'s conversion to Twelver Shi'ism briefly promoted it within state structures. This laid cultural foundations for the later Safavid establishment of Twelver Shi'ism as the state religion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>.</p></div>
